---
id: org.dxos.review.code-style
name: CodeStyle
version: 0.1.0
---

Repo-wide review rules derived from the `code-style` skill — the authoring
conventions a linter cannot express. Each `rule` block below is discovered by the
agentic-review harness, matched against the files its globs select, and applied
by a focused subagent.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                   |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| `rule` | org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0 |

## Rules

```mdl
rule namespace-service-layers: Layer constructors are module-level exports, never class statics
  In a module marked `@import-as-namespace`, the `Context.Service` tag class
  carries the module's own name, so anything hung off it as a `static` forces
  consumers to spell that name twice — `StateStore.StateStore.layerSql` instead
  of `StateStore.layerSql`. Export layer constructors (`layerMemory`,
  `layerSql`, `layer`, …) as module-level `const`s beside the tag, leaving the
  class body empty, and give the service's operations module-level accessors
  built on `Context.Service.use` for the same reason. Flag a `static layer…`
  member on a `Context.Service` subclass, and flag any call site that repeats a
  namespace as `Name.Name.member`. A bare `Name.Name` in a TYPE position is not
  a violation — the tag genuinely is the type, and there is nowhere else to put
  it.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  grep: static layer|([A-Z]\w+)\.\1\.
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-trivial-wrappers-over-official-apis: Do not extract a helper that only forwards to an official API
  A one-line local helper whose body is a single call to a public API
  (`const makeBody = (text: string) => Obj.make(Body, { text })`, and then
  `makeBody('x')` at four call sites) does not remove duplication — it renames
  the API. The reader now has to jump to the definition to learn which API is
  under test and what it was passed, and a file with several of these grows an
  ad-hoc DSL that only its author can read. Inline it: `Obj.make(Body, { text:
  'x' })` at the call site is longer and clearer, and it keeps the API being
  exercised visible in the assertion's own scope. This is most acute in tests,
  where the call being made IS the thing under test, but it applies anywhere.
  Flag a module- or describe-local arrow/function whose body is one expression
  wrapping a single call, adding no branching, no error handling, no derived
  values, and no non-trivial defaults. A helper that composes several calls,
  encodes a non-obvious setup sequence, or is exported for reuse across files
  is NOT a violation, and neither is a named factory that a package publishes
  as its own API (`Obj.make` wrappers such as `File.make`).
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule operations-take-refs-not-ids: Operations identify objects by Ref, never by a raw id string
  An operation input that names an object takes `Ref.Ref(Type)`, not a `string`
  id. A `Ref` carries the type alongside the identity, resolves through the
  database on its own, and still serializes to a DXN — so it crosses an RPC or
  MCP boundary exactly as cheaply as the string it replaces, while a bare
  `spaceId`-less `objectId: string` names nothing in particular and forces every
  handler to re-derive what it points at. Flag an `Operation.make` input field
  whose name ends in `Id`/`Ids` (or is `id`) and whose schema is
  `Schema.String`, where the thing identified is an ECHO object. A `spaceId`, a
  foreign id from an external system (a Gmail message id, a GitHub node id), an
  id inside a stored record rather than an operation input, and an opaque handle
  the operation itself minted are NOT violations.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*Operation.ts
    - packages/**/operations/**/*.ts
  grep: Operation\.make|Id: Schema\.String|Ids: Schema\.Array
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule declare-optional-services-with-noop-layers: An optional service is a declared requirement plus a noop layer, never a callback
  A layer that needs a service only sometimes must still DECLARE it — in the
  `Layer` signature's requirements, and in a `LayerSpec`'s `requires` — and the
  hosts that lack a real implementation must satisfy it with a documented noop
  layer (`layerNoop`). Do not reach for the alternatives: an
  `Effect.serviceOption` read is invisible to a `LayerSpec` stack, where a tag
  the spec does not require is never in its context, so the read comes back
  empty even when the app materialises the service elsewhere; and a
  `getFooManager?: () => Effect<Foo>` callback option moves the wiring out of
  the layer graph into a caller that has to re-derive it, so the graph no longer
  tells you what the layer needs and each host wires it differently — or
  forgets to. The usual justification for both is that requiring the tag would
  prune the provider on a host that does not use the feature; a noop layer
  answers that directly, and fails loudly at the point of use rather than
  silently disabling the feature at construction. Flag an
  `Effect.serviceOption` whose tag is not in the enclosing layer's
  requirements, a `LayerSpec` factory reading a tag absent from its `requires`,
  and an options field typed as a thunk returning a service or an `Effect` of
  one. `Effect.serviceOption` for a genuinely optional *value* (a config
  default, a capability list that may be empty) is NOT a violation, and neither
  is a callback that supplies behaviour rather than a service — a strategy, a
  factory the host parameterises.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
  grep: serviceOption|LayerSpec|\w+\?:.*=>.*(Service|Manager|Provider|Layer)\b
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule import-as-namespace-is-all-or-nothing: A namespace module's directive, filename, re-export and import sites must agree
  The namespace-export style is four things that have to say the same name, and
  a module that satisfies only some of them is worse than one that satisfies
  none — a reader cannot tell from a call site which kind of module they are
  in. (1) The module carries `// @import-as-namespace` directly under the
  copyright header. (2) Its filename IS the namespace: capital-case `Foo.ts`,
  not `foo.ts` or `foo-bar.ts`. (3) The package barrel re-exports it under that
  same name — `export * as Foo from './Foo'`, never a renamed alias. (4) Every
  consumer imports the whole namespace, `import * as Foo from '@dxos/pkg/Foo'`
  or `import { Foo } from '@dxos/pkg'`, and reaches members through it
  (`Foo.make`); a named import of a member (`import { make, type Options } from
  '.../Foo'`) pulls the member out of the namespace that gives it its meaning.
  Inside such a module, members are not prefixed with the namespace's own name
  (`Options`, not `FooOptions` — callers write `Foo.Options` either way) and
  there is no default export. Flag any of the four disagreeing: a directive with
  a lowercase or hyphenated filename, an `export * as X from './Y'` where `X`
  and `Y` differ, a module the barrel exports as a namespace but that carries no
  directive, a member import from a module that does, and a namespace-prefixed
  member inside one. Not violations: the lowercase `*Internal` alias convention
  for internal barrels (`import * as objInternal from './internal/Obj'`), the
  directly-exported `errors.ts` and `testing/` entrypoints, and a module that is
  not a namespace module at all — this rule is about half-applied style, not
  about converting every file.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  grep: @import-as-namespace|export \* as|import \* as
  severity: warn
```

## Appendix

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent to changed files.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files:      Glob | Glob[]     # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?:      Prose             # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?:  warn | error      # defaults to warn
    scope?:     dir | repo        # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
```
